  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Overview
    
    The defining siege of the Orca Incursion, and the engagement that broke Nightfall Shelf as a self-sustaining colony. Zund Gnasharkin, an orca war-leader of the Incursion period, marshaled three war-pods in a coordinated assault on the Shelf's volcanic vents. The siege lasted forty-three days. It ended in mutual catastrophe: the orca commander dead, the colony's fire-cores buried beyond recovery.
    
    The earliest chronicle version gives the duration as "six weeks." Later accounts correct to forty-three days, which the ice-memory corroborates.
    
    ## The Assault
    
    Zund struck during the long twilight, when the aurora stuttered and Nightfall Shelf's crystal-amplifiers could not achieve sufficient resonance to activate the Berg's defensive harmonics. The timing was not instinctive — it required knowledge of the Shelf's operational cycles that no external observer should have possessed.
    
    Three pods hit the thermal vents simultaneously, employing Star∴eternal resonance-patterns — frequencies derived from Aurora Stack harvesting techniques — to crack the fire-barriers from outside. The sources agree that someone disclosed these patterns to the orca. No source names who. The fire-cores began to dim.
    
    ## The Siege
    
    Nightshelf defenders fought in darkness for forty-three days while the colony's children consumed the last reserves of stored heat-water. Defenders employed Hush☽ Twilight, a suppressive silence technique derived from ☽Silv-glaciere☽ Arts, to conceal troop movements through the deep-tunnel corridors. ☽'Netemia Glow-bringer, a high-ranking Nightshelf tunneler and sorcerer, led operations in the lower veins; she carries a scar from direct combat with Zund and has refused to let healers smooth it.
    
    The Threk Boneday binding — a blood-ritual performed by orca shamans during the siege — produced effects that persisted beyond the deaths of the shamans who channeled it. An interrogation record from the aftermath suggests the binding's architects did not fully understand its duration. The ice at the battle site retains impressions consistent with residual ritual presence.
    
    ## Resolution
    
    Weal' Clique~ and Aureate, two merchant-guild factions whose representatives had been arguing over trade-routes three days prior, cast Pod-bond Annihilation in mutual sacrifice, collapsing the Shelf's upper galleries and half the accessible tunnels. Zund Gnasharkin died in the collapse. His pressure-forged trident, Grungruk's Claw, fractured but survived him; recovering it from his grip required four penguins, two of whom did not survive the week.
    
    ## Aftermath
    
    The Shelf still stands. The primary fire-cores still radiate heat somewhere in the rubble, but no one has successfully reignited them. Deep-miners will not descend below the Shattered Vein. The Flipper Accord, the trade agreement binding the Aurora Stack and Nightfall Shelf, holds — but it holds because Aurora pretends not to notice the Shelf's slow dimming, and the Shelf pretends it is not dimming.
    
    The Absconditu Verses cite the Shelf Crushing as proof that openly revealed power invites destruction. They are not wrong. Someone revealed Star∴eternal's frequencies. The Shelf paid for it.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771580423788_0", anchor: "an orca war-leader of the Incursion period", text: "Middling rank. The earliest tunnel-ice at the siege perimeter preserves orca command-songs in which Zund is addressed with subordination harmonics — he answered to someone. Nightshelf combat records from the first week consistently call him pod-lieutenant. \"War-leader\" appears only after the collapse, when survivors needed the thing that broke them to have been led by something extraordinary. I should have caught this in the main text. The habit of inflation is apparently communicable.", type: :correction, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771580423788_1", anchor: "No source names who", text: "Shadow-Broker Nyla's predecessor — an Aurora penguin, despite what the amended histories claim — held office during the siege period. Star∴eternal is an Aurora Stack harvesting technique. The Nightshelf's intelligence apparatus was run by an Aurora defector during the exact window someone handed Aurora-derived resonance patterns to an orca war-pod. Every source I have consulted treats the leak as a mystery. It is not a mystery. It is a question no one wants answered.", type: :skepticism, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771580423788_2", anchor: "pressure-forged trident", text: "Harpoon. The ice-memory and the Nightshelf tunnel-records both say harpoon. \"Grungruk's Clawa\" — singular possessive in orca pidgin, single-pointed weapon. I used \"trident\" because the Aureate chronicle does, and the Aureate chronicle uses \"trident\" because Aureate merchants have seen tridents and have not seen orca pressure-harpoons. Sloppy.", type: :pedantic, display: :popout
